<article class="env-exercise" id="0CT6">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0CT6">Exercise <span data-tag="0CT6">111.18.5</span>.</a> Give an example of a finite, sober, catenary topological space $X$ which does not have a dimension function $\delta : X \to \mathbf{Z}$. Here $\delta : X \to \mathbf{Z}$ is a dimension function if for $x, y \in X$ we have </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$x \leadsto y$ and $x \not= y$ implies $\delta (x) > \delta (y)$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$x \leadsto y$ and $\delta (x) \geq \delta (y) + 2$ implies there exists a $z \in X$ with $x \leadsto z \leadsto y$ and $\delta (x) > \delta (z) > \delta (y)$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> Describe your space clearly and succinctly explain why there cannot be a dimension function. </p>
</article> 
